<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Popular Front of Latvia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1988">1988</a>–<a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Latvijas Tautas fronte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Popular Front of Latvia was a mass movement founded in October 1988 to campaign for Latvian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and, soon, full independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. It won a majority in the Supreme Council elections of March 1990, after which the council declared the restoration of independence on 4 May 1990, and it led the government of Ivars Godmanis during the barricades of January 1991. It declined after independence and was dissolved in 1999.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/popular-front-of-latvia.jpg" alt="Popular Front of Latvia" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Zscout370 at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Latvia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
